1
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:19,610
Radiological emergencies are less common, but no less serious.

2
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:21,010
Here's what you need to do.

3
00:00:21,010 --> 00:00:25,930
If there's a radiological spill or leak, your first job is to isolate the area.

4
00:00:25,930 --> 00:00:31,170
Set up barriers, slap down radiation warning tape, and keep people as far away as possible.

5
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:32,930
Use radiation shields.

6
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:36,530
Lead is the go-to material here, to keep exposure levels down.

7
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:38,170
Wear appropriate PPE.

8
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:42,490
This means lead aprons, gloves, and dosimeters to monitor radiation exposure.

9
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:45,370
You need to be fully suited up for this kind of work.

10
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:48,690
Once everything is contained, decontaminate.

11
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:53,050
Remove contaminated gear carefully, and clean the affected surfaces with the right decontamination

12
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:54,450
agents.

13
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:57,290
Use Geiger counters to ensure the area is safe.

14
00:00:57,290 --> 00:01:02,410
The NRC guidelines for radiological safety, and the ALARA principle, as low as reasonably

15
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:04,890
achievable, are your go-tos.

16
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:06,610
Don't take shortcuts.

17
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:11,090
Radiation exposure can have long-term effects, so you want to minimize it as much as possible.
